WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.750
Smart pointers are the preferred way to express dynamic ownership when value ownership or containers are not enough.

2
00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:12.001
They connect allocation to object lifetime and make cleanup behavior visible in the type.

3
00:00:12.001 --> 00:00:17.251
They also reduce manual cleanup paths that are easy to miss during error handling.

4
00:00:17.251 --> 00:00:21.001
std::unique_ptr is usually the default for exclusive dynamic ownership.

5
00:00:21.001 --> 00:00:24.752
It says that one owner is responsible for the object.

6
00:00:24.752 --> 00:00:28.502
Transfer happens through move semantics, which makes ownership handoff visible.

7
00:00:28.502 --> 00:00:33.002
Destruction is predictable when the unique owner leaves scope or is replaced.

8
00:00:33.002 --> 00:00:38.252
std::shared_ptr is appropriate when multiple owners genuinely need to keep an object alive.

9
00:00:38.252 --> 00:00:43.503
It should represent real shared ownership, not uncertainty about who should own the object.

10
00:00:43.503 --> 00:00:49.503
Shared ownership adds lifecycle complexity because cleanup happens only when the last owner releases its reference.

11
00:00:49.503 --> 00:00:54.004
std::weak_ptr observes an object managed by shared ownership without extending lifetime.

12
00:00:54.004 --> 00:00:59.629
It helps break cycles and lets observers check whether the object still exists before use.

13
00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:04.129
Expired weak observations need clear handling rather than being treated as impossible.

14
00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:09.380
Smart pointers should replace owning raw pointers, but they are not magic security wrappers.

15
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:15.380
They still require clear lifecycle design, consistent construction, documented ownership expectations, and tests around destruction, transfer,

16
00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.880
callbacks, and shutdown behavior.
